# tools/

One TOML file per entry, and the file name is the entry: `tools/ruff.toml` holds the tool
called `ruff`. A name is written as its file name is - lowercase letters, digits, `.`, `_`
and single hyphens, with no hyphen at either end, so `radar add` refuses `MyTool` and says
to enter `mytool` - and no two entries claim one name, ignoring case. `radar gate` FAILs an
entry that breaks either rule, so a file written by hand is held to it too. Everything the
radar knows about a tool lives in its own file, so a change to one entry is a diff a
reviewer can read, and the catalogue has no index to keep in step.

Write one with `radar add`, which validates before it writes rather than after:

```
radar add ruff --repo https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff --axis lint-format --artifact cli --ring observe --license MIT --visibility public --note "Linter and formatter; a candidate for the lint lane" --reason "Entered the catalogue on first look"
```

One line, because a backslash continuation is sh syntax only: PowerShell and cmd read each
line as a new command.

Eight fields are required — `name`, `repo`, `axis`, `ring`, `license`, `visibility`, `note`,
`artifact` — and every entry carries at least one dated `[[history]]` block. `axis` says
which problem the tool addresses; `artifact` says what you install and where it runs, which
is what decides context cost and blast radius; the axes are the catalogue's own when
`radar.toml` declares them under `[entries]`. Run `radar add --help` for the values each
field accepts, and `radar gate` for what an entry has to satisfy beyond its shape:

- every `[[history]]` block carries a `date` (`YYYY-MM-DD`) and a `reason`, and a block at
  `adopt` or `pilot` also carries `evidence` — what the claim of use rests on, which is
  why `radar add` asks for `--evidence` at those rings;
- a `pilot` entry needs `[pilot_exit]` — what would adopt it and what would decline it,
  written BEFORE the data exists, which is the only property that makes it worth anything;
- an `own` or `adopt` entry whose artifact is visible in a session needs a `[telemetry]`
  matcher, because a ring is a claim about observed state and a claim with no instrument is
  not checkable;
- a `public` entry needs `repo` as a URL, because the public projection renders it as a
  link.

Machine-local entries — site-specific tooling whose NAME must not travel — go in `tools.local/`
instead, which is gitignored. The engine loads them, the gate judges them, and the renderer
drops them before writing any tracked file.
